÷2÷Getting_Started_with_a_Short_Tutorial÷Getting Started with a Short Tutorial÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îGetting_Started_with_a_Short_Tutorial

ìIntegrated Development Environment, tutorialì ìtutorial, Integrated Development Environmentì Perhaps the easiest way to understand how the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment manages an entire project's files and tools, is to work through a brief example.  In this walk-through, you create and execute a simple C program using the IDE.  FORTRAN developers can follow along substituting FORTRAN code and file names where appropriate.  The session will help you understand the basic concepts of the IDE and the steps involved in project development.

The illustrations shown in this section are taken from the Windows environment.  The OS/2 environment is very similar with some minor differences.  For example, in the OS/2 environment the IDE uses the OS/2 Editor rather than Open Watcom's Windows Editor.  Although this tutorial builds a Win32 executable, you can choose to build a Win16 or OS/2 PM executable.  The procedures to follow are not that much different.  When the time comes to select the type of target, simply choose an environment that you can run on your machine so that you can run your application to make sure it works.
´
÷3÷A_Simple_Executable÷A Simple Executable÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îA_Simple_Executable

ìIntegrated Development Environment, defining a projectì

Ê
Ì(1)
¹ïbï Double-click on the IDE iconğ in the Open Watcom Tools window.

The Open Watcom IDE Project window appears.  ìnew projectì

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose New Projectğ from the File menu.

The Enter Project Filename dialog appears.  The default project name iscourier12 noname.wpj.helv10

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Enter the filenameğcourier12 \project\firsthelv10 in the File Name field and click on OK.

courier12°c°ide4.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 4.ğïibïğïbï Enter the filename in the Enter Project Filename dialog.ğ

This is the name of the project you are creating.  If you enter a directory that doesn't exist (in this case,courier12 \projecthelv10), the IDE reveals a message box asking if you want to create the specified directory.  Click on OK to create the specified directory and project file.

Ê
ÌNote:
¹ The IDE looks for existing projects with the new name.  If a project with the specified name already exists, the IDE will prompt you rather than simply overwriting the existing project.
Ë

The project name appears in the title bar of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  Note that the IDE added the extension ".wpj" to the project name.  The New Target dialog appears on the screen where you define a target for the new project.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Enter the desired target nameğ in the Target Name field (we suggestcourier12 target1helv10).  Next, select the appropriate Target Environment (we suggest "Win32") and Image Type (we suggest "Character-mode Executable").  Click on OK when completed.

courier12°c°ide5.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 5.ğïibïğïbï Select target name, target environment and image typeğ

The New Target dialog closes and the IDE creates a target window entitledcourier12 target1.exehelv10 for the new target.  Note that the IDE appends the appropriate extension to the target name for you once you click on OK.  You can now add sources to the target.

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Choose New Sourceğ from the Sources menu.

The Add File(s) dialog appears where you specify the sources you want to add to the target.  Source names must be filenames with extensions; pathnames are not required.

For the OS/2 IDE, you can add multiple source files to the target at one time by inserting a space between each filename you enter.  You can also use the file browser to add files by clicking on the Browse...  button.

For all other systems, you can enter the names of the source files (with extensions) in the File Name field or you can use the file browser to select files.  The type of files displayed in file list window can be controlled by modifying the List Files of Type field.  Once you have typed a name in the File Name field or selected a name from the file list, you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button.  All files in the file list can be added by clicking the Add All button.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.

courier12°c°ide6.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 6.ğïibïğïbï Select source file names in the Add File(s) dialogğ

Ì(6)
¹ïbï Enter the following source file names.ğ

courier12      helv10
courier12     source1.c   (or source1.for)helv10
courier12     source2.c   (or source2.for)helv10
courier12     source3.rchelv10

For the OS/2 IDE, enter all the names into the field separating each name by a space.  Click on OK when done.

For all other systems, enter one name at a time into the File Name field, pressing Enter or clicking on Add after each one has been entered.  Click on Close when done.

courier12°c°ide7.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 7.ğïibïğïbï Enter one or several source files in the Add File(s) dialog.ğ

The IDE adds the new source files to the target window, followed by the characters [n/a].  This indicates that the source files are not available, meaning that the files do not yet exist.  The indicators are:

Ê
Ì[n/a]
¹ This code appears if the file does not exist.

Ì[r/o]
¹ This code appears if the file is Read Only.

Ì[sw]
¹ This code appears if the user has set switches for the specified source file.
Ë

The Integrated Development Environment organizes the source files in the target window under headers according to the filename extension.  You can expand and collapse the file groups by clicking on the folder icon.

For this tutorial, we only need one source file, so now we will delete the extra files from the target.

Ì(7)
¹ïbï Click on the fileğcourier12 source2.chelv10 (orcourier12 source2.forhelv10) to select it then choose Remove Source from the Sources menu.

A message box appears asking if you want to remove the selected source.  Click on the Yes button to continue.  The IDE removes the selected source file from the target window.  Now we can removecourier12 source3.rc.helv10  To do this we will perform the function of removing all source files with a common extension.

Ì(8)
¹ïbï Click on the (.rc) headerğ on the target window then choose Remove Source from the Sources menu.

A message box appears on the screen asking if you want to remove all of the ".rc" files.  Click on the Yes button to continue.  The message box closes and the IDE removes thecourier12 source3.rchelv10 file from the target window.  Now you can edit the remaining source file.

Ì(9)
¹ïbï Highlight the lineğcourier12 source1.chelv10 (orcourier12 source1.forhelv10) and choose Edit Text from the Sources menu to edit the selected source file.

The Open Watcom Editor appears.  The name of the selected source file,courier12 source1.chelv10 (orcourier12 source1.forhelv10), appears in the window.  Here you can enter the source code.

ïibïHint:ğ  You can also invoke the Open Watcom Editor by double-clicking on the source file that you want to edit.

In this case, the Open Watcom Editor appears because the selected source file has a ".c" or ".for" extension.  Performing an edit can open different editors, depending on the extension of the selected source file.

Ì(10)
¹ïbï Enter the following lines of source code.ğ

Ê
ÌImportant:
¹ For the purpose of this session, we ask you to enterïbï iiğ instead ofïbï iğ in the "print" line.  This will allow you to see how the Integrated Development Environment reacts to errors.  You will then be able to correct the error.
Ë

courier12      helv10
courier12     #include <stdio.h>helv10

courier12     void main()helv10
courier12     {helv10
courier12         int i;helv10
courier12         for (i=0; i<10; i++)helv10
courier12         {helv10
courier12             printf( "Value is %d\n", ii );helv10
courier12         }helv10
courier12         printf("Press Enter...\n");helv10
courier12         getchar();helv10
courier12     }helv10

FORTRAN programmers can enter the following lines of code.

courier12      helv10
courier12           program mainhelv10
courier12           implicit nonehelv10
courier12           integer ihelv10

courier12           do i = 0, 9helv10
courier12             print *, 'Value is ', iihelv10
courier12           enddohelv10

courier12           print *, 'Press Enter...'helv10
courier12           read *helv10

courier12           endhelv10

You can now save your work and close the Editor in one of the following two ways:  close down the entire editor or close down only the source file window.  For this exercise, close only the source file window as follows.

Ì(11)
¹ïbï Choose Closeğ from the File menu to close down the current source file window.

In both cases, if changes have been made to the file and not saved, a message prompts you to save the changes before exiting.  The advantage of closing only the Editor window is that the Editor continues to run.  If you need to access it again, you will not need to wait for it to go through the start-up procedures again.

Ê
ÌImportant:
¹ You must save the file.  The Open Watcom compilers do not access the source lines in the Editor's buffers.
Ë

Ì(12)
¹ïbï Minimize the Editor window.ğ

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Click on the Open Watcom IDE Project windowğ to bring it to the foreground.

Ì(13)
¹ïbï Choose Refreshğ from the Window menu.

This action forces the IDE to update the display with the state of all files.  ìauto refreshì It is not necessary to use this function if you have selected Auto Refresh in the File menu.  If Auto Refresh is disabled then it may be necessary to alert the IDE of the presence of the file on disk.  ìsave projectì

Ì(14)
¹ïbï Choose Save Projectğ from the File menu to save your project up to this point.

You are now ready to make and run the target.  Making a target is the act of constructing a target file.  In this case, it involves compiling the source filecourier12 source1.chelv10 (orcourier12 source1.forhelv10) using the appropriate Open Watcom compiler and linking the target executable using the Open Watcom Linker.

Ì(15)
¹ïbï Highlight the lineğcourier12 source1.chelv10 and choose C Compiler Switches...  from the Source Options item located under the Sources menu to set compile options.

ïbïFORTRAN programmers:ğ  Highlight the linecourier12 source1.forhelv10 and choose Fortran Compiler Switches...  from the Source Options item located under the Sources menu to set compile options.

The C Compiler Switches dialog appears.  If you are using FORTRAN, the Fortran Compiler Switches dialog appears.

Ì(16)
¹ïbï Click on the ">>" buttonğ until thecourier12 7.  CodeGen Strategy Switcheshelv10 dialog appears.  Click on the "Multithreaded application [-bm]" checkbox button to select this option.

ïbïFORTRAN programmers:ğ  Click on the ">>" button until thecourier12 9.  Application Type Switcheshelv10 dialog appears.  Click on the "Multithreaded application [-bm]" checkbox to select this option.

A check-mark appears in the checkbox.

Note:  Selecting this option is for demonstration purposes only, it is not required for this simple application.

Click OK to close the dialog.

Ì(17)
¹ïbï Choose Makeğ from the Targets menu.

The IDE constructs a makefile based on its knowledge of the target and construction rules and then invokes the Open Watcom Make utility to createcourier12 target1.exe.helv10  The IDE Log window appears at the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  Use this window to monitor the procedure.  Since you enteredïbï iiğ instead ofïbï i,ğ the MAKE fails.

courier12°c°ide8.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 8.ğïibïğïbï Use the IDE Log window to monitor the make procedure.ğ

Ì(18)
¹ïbï Highlight the error messageğ in the IDE Log window and choose Help on Message from the Log menu.
Help information regarding the error message appears on the screen.

courier12°c°ide9.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 9.ğïibïğïbï Help for error messages is availableğ

Close the Help window and edit the source file as follows.

Ì(19)
¹ïbï Highlight the error messageğ in the IDE Log window and choose Edit File from the Log menu.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Double-click on the error messageğ in the IDE Log window.

The Open Watcom Editor opens and the cursor appears at the position of the error.  Edit and correct the error and then save the file again.  Minimize the Editor window and bring the IDE window to the foreground.

Ì(20)
¹ïbï Choose Makeğ from the Targets menu again.

This time, the procedure creates the executable filecourier12 target1.exe.helv10

Ì(21)
¹ïbï Choose Runğ from the Targets menu.

The IDE executes the programcourier12 target1.exe.helv10

courier12°c°ide10.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 10.ğïibïğïbï Run from the Targets menu runs the target.ğ

The program you wrote is a character mode application and its output appears in a separate console window.  You are now ready to debug your target program.  You probably do not need to debug this simple program with the Open Watcom Debugger but go through the steps so that you know what to do for more complex programs.

The IDE has several debugging information options which it uses as defaults when it is started.  The first thing to do is check to make sure that your application compiled with the default debugging information activated.

Ì(22)
¹ïbï Choose Target Optionsğ from the Targets menu to activate the Target Options menu.

The option Use Development Switches should appear in grey text with a check mark next to it.  Development Switches automatically include debugging information in the target.  If you were to select Use Release Switches, the IDE would select options useful for a production version of your program.  Selecting this option turns the text grey and places a check mark beside it.  The IDE uses the development switches by default.

Ê
ÌNote:
¹ For the purpose of this session, you should make the target with the development switches on.  If this is not the option selected, select it and remake the target before debugging.
Ë

Ì(23)
¹ïbï Choose Debugğ from the Targets menu.

The IDE starts the Open Watcom Debugger.

Ì(24)
¹ïbï Choose Step Overğ from the Run menu of the Open Watcom Debugger to step through the code.  Chooseïbï Goğ from the Run menu of the Open Watcom Debugger to continue execution to the end of the code.  For more information on using the Open Watcom Debugger, refer to theïibï Open Watcom Debugger User's Guide.ğïbï  The task has completed runningğ appears when the program has completed execution.

Ì(25)
¹ïbï Choose Exitğ from the File menu to terminate the debugging session.

The Open Watcom Debugger closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now exit the IDE.

Ì(26)
¹ïbï Choose Exitğ from the File menu to exit the IDE.

The IDE prompts you to save the current project if you have not saved it since the last change.
Ë
