÷2÷Working_with_Targets÷Working with Targets÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îWorking_with_Targets

ìIntegrated Development Environment, targetsì ìtargetsì Once you have created a project, it is a simple matter to add additional targets.  You can also change the name of a target, remove targets, change the target environment, and change the image type.  This section discusses the following topics:

Òc
Ó Adding a Target

Ó Minimizing a Target

Ó Removing a Target

Ó Renaming a Target

Ó Marking a Target for Remake

Ó Making Targets

Ó Running a Target
Ô
´
÷3÷Adding_a_Target÷Adding a Target÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îAdding_a_Target

ìIntegrated Development Environment, adding a targetì ìtargets, addingì You add targets to the current project using the New Target dialog.  When you create a new project, this dialog appears automatically.  You have two options when adding targets to a project:  you can either enter the name of a new target or you can choose an existing target, previously created in another project.  This section describes both methods for adding a target.

Ê
ÌTo add a new target to the current project:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose New Targetğ from the Targets menu.

The New Target dialog appears on the screen where you enter the details of the target you are adding.

courier12°c°ide13.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 13.ğïibïğïbï Enter the new target details in the New Target dialog.ğ

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Enter the target nameğ in the Target Name field.

Enter the entire pathname to ensure the directory is created where you want.  Depending on the nature of the target, it may or may not be able to share the same directory as other pre-existing targets.  Generally, if the target shares one or more of the same source files as other targets in the project, then it must be placed in its own subdirectory.  The object files that are produced for the target may have different attributes than a pre-existing target's object files (e.g., 16-bit vs 32-bit).  This is why the target should be placed in a separate subdirectory.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Select the desired Target Environment and Image Typeğ as desired.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ when the Target Name, Target Environment and Image Type fields are correct.

If you specify a new directory in the target pathname, a message box appears asking if you want to create the specified directory.  Choose Yes or No to continue.  A window representing the new target appears on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  The target name appears in the title bar.

The first line in this window describes the target.  It specifies the current mode, the target type.  The current mode is represented by either the letter D or R which appears in parentheses in front of the target type.  The letter D indicates that the target is placed in Development mode.  The letter R indicates that the target is placed in Release mode.  The rest of the window displays the source files for the target.
Ë

courier12°c°ide14.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 14.ğïibïğïbï A project may have multiple targets.ğ

Ê
ÌTo add an existing target to a current project:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose New Targetğ from the Targets menu.

The New Target dialog appears on the screen.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Click on the Browse button.ğ

The Enter Target Filename dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog allows you to browse the directories and files and select the ".tgt" file for the target you want to add.

courier12°c°ide15.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 15.ğïibïğïbï Choose an existing target from the Pick Target Filename dialog for the current project.ğ

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Click on the desired targetğ to select it.

The selected target appears in the File Name field.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ to process the target selection.

The Enter Target Filename dialog closes.

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ to close the New Target dialog.

A window representing the new target appears on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  The target name appears in the title bar.

The first line in this window describes the target.  It specifies the current mode and the target type.  The current mode is represented by either the letter D or R which appears in parentheses in front of the target type.  The letter D indicates that the target is placed in development mode.  The letter R indicates that the target is placed in Release mode.  The rest of the window displays the source files for the target.
Ë
´
÷3÷Minimizing_a_Target÷Minimizing a Target÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îMinimizing_a_Target

ìIntegrated Development Environment, minimizing a targetì ìminimizing a targetì If you are working with a project that has multiple targets, the Open Watcom IDE Project window can become cluttered.  You can tidy your window by minimizing the target windows that you are not currently using.

Ê
ÌTo minimize a target window:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click on the system boxğ in the upper left-hand corner of the target window you want to close.

A menu appears listing the functions you can perform on the selected window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Minimizeğ from the menu.

The selected target window closes and an icon appears along the bottom of the screen representing that window.  The name of the target appears on or under the icon.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Double-click on the iconğ to open the target window again.
Ë

courier12°c°ide16.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 16.ğïibïğïbï Minimize targets to reduce clutter in the project window.ğ
