÷3÷Removing_a_Target÷Removing a Target÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îRemoving_a_Target

ìIntegrated Development Environment, removing a targetì ìremoving a targetì The procedure for removing a target from a project is simple.  If your project has multiple targets, it is important to ensure that the target you want to remove is the one selected.  Removing a target does not permanently delete the target, it simply removes it from the current project.  If the target is attached to multiple projects, those projects will not be affected by the removal of the selected target from the current project.

Ê
ÌTo remove a target from the current project:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the target windowğ you want to remove from the project.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Remove Targetğ from the Targets menu.

A message box appears on the screen asking if you really want to remove the selected target.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose OKğ to continue.

The message box closes and the selected target window disappears from the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
Ë
´
÷3÷Renaming_a_Target÷Renaming a Target÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îRenaming_a_Target

ìIntegrated Development Environment, renaming a targetì ìrenaming a targetì When renaming a target, you can either give it a new name or you can give it the name of an existing target.  Choosing an existing target name overwrites the contents of the existing target with the contents of the current target.

Ê
ÌTo rename a target:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the target windowğ you want to rename.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Rename Targetğ from the Targets menu.

The Rename Target dialog appears.  The Target Name, Target Environment, and Image Type of the selected target appear in the appropriate fields.

courier12°c°ide17.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 17.ğïibïğïbï Change a target's name using the Rename Target dialog.ğ

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Enter the new target nameğ in the Target Name field.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Click on the Browse buttonğ and select an existing target name; click on OK when completed.

The selected or entered target name appears in the Target Name field.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Verify that the Target Environment and Image Type are correct.ğ

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ on the Rename Target window.

The Rename Target dialog closes.  The title bar of the selected target window now displays the new target name.
Ë
´
÷3÷Marking_a_Target_for_Remake÷Marking a Target for Remake÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îMarking_a_Target_for_Remake

ìIntegrated Development Environment, marking a targetì ìmarking a targetì Marking a target for remake ensures that the IDE will perform the link the next time you make that target or make all targets.  This function is necessary if you are not using the Autodepend function in the Setup Target submenu under the Targets menu.  By marking a target for remake, you control when and which targets need to be remade.

Ê
ÌTo mark a target for remake:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the target windowğ you want to mark.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Mark Target for Remakeğ from the Targets menu.

A message box appears asking if you want to also mark all of the source files of the selected target.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose Yes or Noğ to continue.

Choosing Yes marks all of the source files for the selected target as well as the target itself.  This means that the next time you do a make, the source files for the selected target will be compiled.  Choosing No marks only the selected target for remake.  In both cases, the message box closes and you return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
Ë
´
÷3÷Making_Targets÷Making Targets÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îMaking_Targets

ìIntegrated Development Environment, making a targetì ìmaking a targetì The Make function in the Targets menu creates an executable for the selected target.  When you perform this function, the IDE compiles the source files for the selected target into object files.  The object files are then linked together to create the target file.

There are two make functions available to you for targets:  Make and Make All.  The Make All function in the Actions menu tells the IDE to compile and link all of the targets for the current project.  This can be a lengthy process, depending upon the number of targets in your project, so you may choose to only make targets to which changes have been made.  This enables you to see if they can be made successfully.

Performing the Make function opens the IDE Log window which allows you to monitor the progress of the make.

Ê
ÌTo make a single target:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the target windowğ you want to make to select it.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Makeğ from the Targets menu.

The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.ïbï  Execution Completeğ appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the selected target.  If the make is successful an executable file for the selected target can be found in the directory associated with the target window.

courier12°c°ide18.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 18.ğïibïğïbï Use the IDE Log window to monitor the make procedure.ğ

Ë

Ê
ÌTo make all targets for the current project:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Make Allğ from the Actions Menu.

The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.ïbï  Execution Completeğ appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the targets.  If the make is successful an executable file for each target in the project can be found in the directory associated with the target window.
Ë
´
÷4÷Viewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Target÷Viewing the Make Command for a Target÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îViewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Target

ìIntegrated Development Environment, viewing make commandsì ìviewing make commandsì With the IDE you can use the Show Make Command function to display the makefile commands for the selected target.  These are the commands that will be used to compile the selected target when you choose the function Make from the Targets menu or Make All from the Actions menu.

Ê
ÌTo view the make commands for targets:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the target windowğ whose makefile commands you want to view.

The title bar of the selected target window is highlighted.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Target Optionsğ from the Targets menu.

A submenu appears on the screen from the Target Options menu.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose Show Make Commandğ from the Target Options submenu.

The Target Command dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected target.

courier12°c°ide19.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 19.ğïibïğïbï The Target Command dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected target.ğ

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ to close the dialog.
Ë
´
