´
÷3÷Setting_Application_Parameters÷Setting Application Parameters÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îSetting_Application_Parameters

ìIntegrated Development Environment, setting optionsì ìsetting optionsì The Targets and Sources menus and their submenus are divided into sections.  Horizontal lines separate each section.  The menu items in the bottom sections of the Targets and Sources menus are actions that you can perform on the selected target or source file in the current project.  If there are switches that can be set for these actions, there are corresponding menu item entries in the bottom sections of the submenus.  Choose Target Options and Source Options from their respective menus to display the submenus.
´
÷2÷Working_with_Source_Files÷Working with Source Files÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îWorking_with_Source_Files

ìIntegrated Development Environment, source filesì ìsource filesì Once your project has a target you need to add source files to the target.  The procedure for adding a source file to a target is simple.  Once you have source files that make up your target, you can also change the name of a source file and remove source files.  This section discusses the following topics:

Òc
Ó Adding Source Files

Ó Removing Source Files

Ó Renaming a Source File

Ó Marking a Source File for Remake

Ó Making Source Files

Ó Viewing Included Files
Ô
´
÷3÷Adding_Source_Files÷Adding Source Files÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îAdding_Source_Files

ìIntegrated Development Environment, adding source filesì ìsource files, addingì You add source files to the selected target (the target whose title bar is currently highlighted) using the Add File(s) dialog.  The current project must have a target in order to use the New Source function.  You have two options when adding source files to a target:  you can either enter the name of a new source file or you can choose an existing source file.  When adding existing source files you can use the file browser, type sources one at a time into the Add File(s) dialog, or use wild cards in the Add File(s) dialog.  This section describes all methods for adding source files.

The extension you use with the source file name determines the compiler that the IDE uses to create the target.  For example, the ".c" extension, invokes the Open Watcom C compiler to make the target.  You can specify several different compilers:

Ê
Ìxxx.asm
¹ ìextensions, .asmì The ".asm" extension invokes the Open Watcom Assembler.

Ìxxx.c
¹ ìextensions, .cì The ".c" extension invokes the Open Watcom C compiler.

Ìxxx.cpp
¹ ìextensions, .cppì The ".cpp" extension invokes the Open Watcom C++ compiler.

Ìxxx.for
¹ ìextensions, .forì The ".for" extension invokes the Open Watcom FORTRAN 77 compiler.

Ìxxx.hlp
¹ ìextensions, .hlpì The ".hlp" extension invokes either the Win16, OS/2, or Win32 compiler.

Ìxxx.rc
¹ ìextensions, .rcì The ".rc" extension invokes the Open Watcom resource compiler.

Ìxxx.res
¹ ìextensions, .resì The ".res" extension invokes the Open Watcom Resource Editor.

Ìxxx.sqc
¹ ìextensions, .sqcì The ".sqc" extension invokes the embedded SQL preprocessor and the Open Watcom C compiler.

Ìxxx.sqp
¹ ìextensions, .sqpì The ".sqp" extension invokes the embedded SQL preprocessor and the Open Watcom C++ compiler.
Ë

Ê
ÌTo add new source files to the selected target:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose New Sourceğ from the Sources menu.

The Add File(s) dialog appears where you specify the name(s) of the source file(s) you are adding.  Source names must be filenames with extensions; pathnames are not required.

For the OS/2 IDE, you can add multiple source files to the target at one time by inserting a space between each filename you enter into the filenames field.

For all other systems, you can enter the names of the source files (with extensions) in the File Name field, one at a time.  Once you have typed a name in the File Name field, you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.

courier12°c°ide22.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 22.ğïibïğïbï Add one or several source files to a target using the Add File(s) dialog.ğ

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Enter the source file name(s).ğ

For the OS/2 IDE, enter all the names into the field separating each name by a space.  Click on OK when done.

For all other systems, enter one name at a time into the File Name field, pressing Enter or clicking on Add after each one has been entered.  Click on Close when done.

The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.

The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order.  You can expand and collapse the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header.
Ë

Ê
ÌTo add existing source files to the selected target using the file browser:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose New Sourceğ from the Sources menu.

The Add File(s) dialog appears on the screen.

For the OS/2 IDE, click on the Browse button and the Pick Filename dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog allows you to browse the directories and files and select the source file(s) you want to add to the current target.  Click on the desired source file to select it.  The selected source file(s) appear highlighted in the Pick Filename dialog.  When done browsing files, click on OPEN to add the selected files.  The Pick Filename dialog closes.  When done selecting files, click on OK.

For all other systems, you can use the file browser to select files.  The type of files displayed in file list window can be controlled by modifying the List Files of Type field.  Once you have selected a name from the file list, you can add it to the list of source files to be included by clicking the Add button.  All files in the file list can be added by clicking the Add All button.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.  When done, click on Close to add the selected files.

The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.

The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order.  You can expand and collapse the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header.
Ë

Ê
ÌTo add existing source files to the selected target using wild cards:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose New Sourceğ from the Sources menu.

The Add File(s) dialog appears on the screen.

For the OS/2 IDE, enter the source file name pattern in the filename(s) field using wild cards.  For example, entercourier12 sou*.chelv10 to add all source files whose name begins "sou" and have a ".c" extension to the current target.  When done, click on OK to add the selected files.

ïibïHint:ğ  Even when entering wild cards, you can add multiple source file groups by separating each entry with one space (e.g., sou*.c sou*.cpp).

For all other systems, enter the source file name pattern in the File Name field using wild cards.  For example, entercourier12 sou*.chelv10 to select all source files whose name begins "sou" and have a ".c" extension.  Click on the Add All button to add all the names that match the wild card pattern.  Individual names can be removed from the "add" list by selecting them and clicking the Remove button.  When done, click on Close to add the selected files.

The Add File(s) dialog closes and the selected source file(s) appear in the selected target window on the Open Watcom IDE Project window.

The source files are grouped by their file extensions and appear in alphabetical order.  You can expand and collapse the list of source files by clicking on the folder icon to the left of the header.
Ë
´
÷3÷Removing_a_Source_File÷Removing a Source File÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îRemoving_a_Source_File

ìIntegrated Development Environment, removing source filesì ìsource files, removingì The procedure for removing a source file from a project is simple.  You can remove one source at a time or you can remove all source files with a common file extension from the target.  Removing a source file does not permanently delete the it.  The IDE simply removes it from the current target.  If the removed source file is common to multiple targets, the other targets will not be affected by the removal of the selected source file.

Ê
ÌTo remove one source file from a target:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the source fileğ you want to remove from the target.

The selected source file appears highlighted in the target window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Remove Sourceğ from the Sources menu.

A message box appears asking if you are sure you want to remove the selected source file.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Click on the Yes button.ğ

The message box closes and the IDE removes the selected source file from the target window.
Ë

Ê
ÌTo remove a group of source files from a target:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the headerğ to the right of the folder icon of the source files group you want to remove from the target.

The selected header appears highlighted in the target window.

courier12°c°ide23.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 23.ğïibïğïbï Click on the header to remove all source files with a common file extension.ğ

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Remove Sourceğ from the Sources menu.

A message window appears asking if you want to remove all source files with the selected file extension.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose OKğ to continue.

The message box closes and the IDE removes the selected group of source files from the target.
Ë
´
÷3÷Renaming_a_Source_File÷Renaming a Source File÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îRenaming_a_Source_File

ìIntegrated Development Environment, renaming source filesì ìsource files, renamingì When renaming a source file, you can either give it a new name or you can give it the name of an existing source file.  Choosing an existing source file name overwrites the contents of that source file with the contents of the current source file.

Ê
ÌTo rename a source file:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the source fileğ you want to rename.

The selected source file appears highlighted in the target window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Rename Sourceğ from the Sources menu.

The Rename File dialog appears where you enter the new name of the selected source file.

courier12°c°ide24.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 24.ğïibïğïbï Use the Rename File dialog to rename the selected source file.ğ

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Enter the new source file nameğ in the field.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Click on the Browse buttonğ and select an existing source file name; click on OK when completed.

The selected or entered source file name appears in the Rename File dialog.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ on the Rename File dialog.

The Rename File dialog closes.  The target window displays the updated source file.
Ë
´
÷3÷Marking_a_Source_File_for_Remake÷Marking a Source File for Remake÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îMarking_a_Source_File_for_Remake

ìIntegrated Development Environment, marking source files for remakeì ìsource files, marking for remakeì The Mark Source for Remake function in the Sources menu enables you to mark a source file to ensure that the IDE will recompile it the next time you make the target.  This function is useful because although changes may not have been made to the selected source file, changes may have been made to a source file on which the selected source file depends.  This function is necessary if you are not using the Autodepend function in the Target Options submenu under the Targets menu.  Marking source files for remake gives you control over when and which source files need to be remade.

With the Mark function you can mark an individual source file or a group of source files with a common file extension.

Ê
ÌTo mark a source file or a group of source files for remake:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the source fileğ you want to mark.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Click once on the headerğ of the group of source files you want to mark.

The selection is highlighted in the target window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Mark Source for Remakeğ from the Sources menu.

If you selected a group of source files to mark, a message box appears asking if you want to also mark all of the source files of the specified extension.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose Yesğ to continue.

The message box disappears and the IDE marks all of the source files in the selected group to be recompiled.
Ë
´
÷3÷Making_Source_Files÷Making Source Files÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îMaking_Source_Files

ìIntegrated Development Environment, making source filesì ìsource files, makingì The Make function in the Sources menu enables you to compile a selected source file into an object file.  You can also perform the Make function on a group of source files with a common file extension.

Performing the Make function opens the IDE Log window which allows you to monitor the progress of the make.

Ê
ÌTo make source files:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the source fileğ you want to make.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Click once on the headerğ of the group of source files you want to make.

The selected source files are highlighted in the target window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Makeğ from the Sources menu.

The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.ïbï  Execution Completeğ appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the selected source files.
Ë

Ê
ÌTo make all targets for the current project:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Make Allğ from the Actions Menu.

The IDE Log window opens along the bottom of the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  In the log window you can monitor the make procedure.ïbï  Execution Completeğ appears in the IDE Log window when the make is completed on the targets.  If the make is successful an executable file for each target in the project can be found in the directory associated with the target window.
Ë
´
÷4÷Viewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Source_File÷Viewing the Make Command for a Source File÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îViewing_the_Make_Command_for_a_Source_File

ìIntegrated Development Environment, viewing make commandsì ìviewing make commandsì With the IDE you can use the Show Make Command function to display the makefile commands for the selected source file.  These are the commands that will be used to compile the selected source file when you choose the function Make from the Sources menu.

Ê
ÌTo view the make commands for source files:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the source fileğ whose makefile commands you want to view.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Click once on the headerğ of the group of source files whose makefile commands you want to view.

The selected source file or group header is highlighted.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Source Optionsğ from the Sources menu.

A submenu appears on the screen from the Source Options menu.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose Show Make Commandğ from the Source Options submenu.

The Source Command dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected source file or source file header.

courier12°c°ide25.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 25.ğïibïğïbï The IDE Source Command dialog displays the makefile commands for the selected source file(s).ğ

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ to close the dialog.
Ë
´
÷3÷Viewing_Included_Files÷Viewing Included Files÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îViewing_Included_Files

ìIntegrated Development Environment, viewing included files listì ìviewing included files listì The Included Files function in the Sources menu enables you to select a source file and view a list of all of the files included by that source file.  This is a list of all files on which the selected source file depends.  From this list you can select included files and edit them.

Because the selected source file depends on the files in this list, it is necessary to remake it if changes are made to any of the included files.  If you are using the Autodepend function, the IDE automatically remakes the source file if any of the included files are modified.

Ê
ÌTo view the list of included files:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click on the source fileğ whose list of included files you want to view.

The selected source file is highlighted in the target window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Included Filesğ from the Sources menu.

The Files Included dialog appears on the screen.  This dialog lists all source files on which the selected source files depends.
Ë

Ê
ÌTo edit a source file from the File Included dialog:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the source fileğ in the included files list that you want to edit.

The selected source file is highlighted in the File Included dialog.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Click on OK.ğ

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Double-clickğ on the selected source file.

The Files Included dialog closes and the IDE invokes the appropriate editor for the selected included file.  The editor appears and you can edit the file.  Close the editor when you are finished making the desired changes to return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.
Ë
´
÷2÷Using_the_IDE_Log_Window÷Using the IDE Log Window÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îUsing_the_IDE_Log_Window

ìIntegrated Development Environment, using the log windowì ìusing the log windowì ìlog window, using theì When you compile a target or source file, the IDE Log window appears along the bottom of the screen.  Once this window is open it remains on your screen until you close the project.  However, to maximize your project window space you can minimize the IDE Log window when it is not being used.

The Log menu contains three functions that you use in relation to the IDE Log window.  This section discusses the following functions:

Òc
Ó Stopping the Make Function

Ó Getting Help

Ó Editing the Source File

Ó Saving the Log
Ô
´
÷3÷Stopping_the_Make_Function÷Stopping the Make Function÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îStopping_the_Make_Function

ìIntegrated Development Environment, stopping the makeì ìstopping the makeì Choosing to make a source file, target, or all targets can be a lengthy process.  The Stop function in the Log menu gives you the opportunity to cancel the make function once it has started.  This is a useful function in case you decide that you do not want to continue with the make.

Ê
ÌTo stop the make function:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Stopğ from the Log menu.

A message box appears asking if it is okay to interrupt the executing process.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ to continue.

The message box closes and the IDE stops the make function.  The IDE Log window displays the messageïbï Stop Invokedğ followed by the messageïbï Execution Complete.ğ
Ë
´
÷3÷Getting_Help÷Getting Help÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îGetting_Help

ìIntegrated Development Environment, getting helpì ìgetting helpì The Help on Message function in the Log menu gives you access to on-line help information for the selected error message in the IDE Log window.

Ê
ÌTo reveal help for an error message:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the error messageğ in the IDE Log window on which you want information.

The selected error message is highlighted in the IDE Log window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Help on Messageğ from the Log menu.

This opens the Help window.  Information on the selected error message appears in this window.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose Closeğ from the system menu on the Help window to cancel the Help facility.

The Help window closes and you return to the IDE Log window.
Ë
´
÷3÷Editing_the_Source_File÷Editing the Source File÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îEditing_the_Source_File

ìIntegrated Development Environment, editing sourceì ìediting sourceì ìsource files, editingì The Edit File function in the Log menu allows you to select an error message in the IDE Log window and edit the corresponding source file.  This function invokes the appropriate editor for the source file and positions your cursor in the source code where the error occurred (if the error message contained line and column information).

Ê
ÌTo edit a source file:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the error messageğ in the IDE Log window that you want to edit.

The selected error message is highlighted in the IDE Log window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Edit Fileğ from the Log menu.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Double-clickğ on the selected error message.

Both of these actions open the appropriate editor.  If the Open Watcom Editor appears and the error message contained column and line details, your cursor will be positioned at the error in the source code.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Make the required changesğ to the source file and exit the Editor.
Ë
´
÷3÷Saving_the_Log÷Saving the Log÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îSaving_the_Log

ìIntegrated Development Environment, saving the logì ìsaving the logì The Save Log As function in the Log menu allows you to save the contents of the Log window into a file.

Ê
ÌTo save the log:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Save Log Asğ from the Log menu.

The Save Log As dialog appears where you specify the filename by which you want to save the contents of the Log window.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Enter the desired log nameğ in the File Name field.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Specify the directoryğ to which you want to save the log.

