÷3÷Using_the_Autodepend_Switch÷Using the Autodepend Switch÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îUsing_the_Autodepend_Switch

ìIntegrated Development Environment, autodepend switchì ìswitches, autodependì The Use Autodepend switch in the Target Options submenu of the Targets menu keeps track of updated source files.  Once a source is modified, the IDE remembers that it needs to be recompiled the next time you do a make.  By using the Use Autodepend switch, the IDE also keeps track of changes made to source files on which other source files depend.

For example, let's say that your current project has a source file,courier12 source.cpp,helv10 that depends on a header file,courier12 source.hpp.helv10  If the Use Autodepend feature is not enabled and changes are made tocourier12 source.hpphelv10 aftercourier12 source.cpphelv10 in your current project has been compiled, the IDE does not recognize that your current source file is now out of date.  This means that the next time you do a make, the IDE will not compilecourier12 source.cpp.helv10  The Use Autodepend feature tells the IDE to compare the date and time stamps on all of the dependent source files in addition to the source files in the current project and their corresponding object files, so the IDE recognizes when your current source file needs to be recompiled.

Ê
ÌTo set the Use Autodepend switch:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Target Optionsğ from the Targets menu.

A submenu appears from the Target Options menu.  A check mark beside the menu option indicates that it is enabled.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Select the Use Autodepend optionğ from the Target Options submenu.

If the option was enabled when you selected it, you have disabled the Use Autodepend feature.  If the option had no check mark when you selected it, you have enabled the Use Autodepend feature.
Ë
´
÷2÷Configuring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment÷Configuring the Integrated Development Environment÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îConfiguring_the_Integrated_Development_Environment

ìIntegrated Development Environment, configurationì ìconfiguring the IDEì ìIntegrated Development Environment, customizationì ìcustomizing the IDEì The Integrated Development Environment can be reconfigured to some extent.  You can customize the following features.

Òc
Ó Show or hide the Toolbar

Ó Show or hide the Status Bar

Ó Enable or disable automatic updates of file status in the target windows

Ó Select your own text editor

Ó Select a source revision control system
Ô

The next few sections describe these features.
´
÷3÷Show_Toolbar÷Show Toolbar÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îShow_Toolbar

ìshow tool barì The Integrated Development Environment Toolbar that appears under the Menu Bar can be removed from or added to the IDE window.

Ê
ÌTo add or remove the toolbar:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Fileğ from the menu bar.

A checkmark beside Show Tool Bar indicates that the toolbar is present on the screen.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Select Show Tool Bar.ğ

The Show Tool Bar state will be toggled.  If the toolbar was on the screen, it will disappear.  If the toolbar was not on the screen, it will reappear.
Ë
´
÷3÷Show_Status_Bar÷Show Status Bar÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îShow_Status_Bar

ìshow status barì The Integrated Development Environment Status Bar that appears at the bottom of the IDE window can be removed from or added to the screen.

Ê
ÌTo add or remove the status bar:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Fileğ from the menu bar.

A checkmark beside Show Status Bar indicates that the status bar is present on the screen.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Select Show Status Bar.ğ

The Show Status Bar state will be toggled.  If the status bar was on the screen, it will disappear.  If the status bar was not on the screen, it will reappear.
Ë
´
÷3÷Auto_Refresh÷Auto Refresh÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îAuto_Refresh

ìauto refreshì The IDE will automatically update the target windows with the state of all files when this option is enabled.

Ê
ÌTo enable or disable auto refresh:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Fileğ from the menu bar.

A checkmark beside Auto Refresh indicates that auto refresh is enabled.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Select Auto Refresh.ğ

The Auto Refresh state will be toggled.  When Auto Refresh is disabled, it may be necessary to select Refresh from the Window menu whenever the target window status becomes out of date.
Ë
´
÷3÷Selecting_Your_Own_Text_Editor÷Selecting Your Own Text Editor÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îSelecting_Your_Own_Text_Editor

ìset text editorì You can use your own favourite text editor from within the Integrated Development Environment.

Ê
ÌTo select your own text editor:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Set Text Editorğ from the File menu.

The Set Text Editor dialog appears.  You can enter the name of the text editor in the first field.  You must also indicate whether the text editor is an executable file or a Dynamic Link Library (DLL).  If the editor is an executable file (rather than a DLL), then you can enter an argument line in the second field.  The argument line will be supplied to the editor whenever it is started by the IDE.  The argument line can include any of three different macros which will be filled in by the IDE.  The macros are:

Ê
Ì%f
¹ The name of the file to be edited.

Ì%r
¹ The row in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a line number then the row value is extracted from the message; otherwise the row value is 1.

Ì%c
¹ The column in the file at which to position the cursor.  If the editor is invoked from a diagnostic message which contains a column number then the column value is extracted from the message; otherwise the column value is 1.
Ë

For example, if the editor argument line that you specified was:

courier12      helv10
courier12     file='%f' row='%r' col='%c'helv10

and you double click on an error message in the Log window that names the filecourier12 foobar.chelv10 with an error at line 215 and column 31, then the argument line that is passed to your editor is:

courier12      helv10
courier12     file='foobar.c' row='215' col='31'helv10

This flexibility allows you to specify the name of the file to edit and the row and/or column at which to position the text cursor.  If no row or column is available, then the IDE will supply the value of 1 as a default.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Select OKğ when you wish to confirm the selection of a new editor.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Select Cancelğ when you wish to cancel the selection of a new editor.

or

Ì 
¹ïbï Select Defaultğ when you wish to restore the default editor selection and then select OK or Cancel.
Ë
´
÷3÷Selecting_a_Source_Revision_Control_System÷Selecting a Source Revision Control System÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îSelecting_a_Source_Revision_Control_System

ìset source controlì ìRCSì You can use a source code Revision Control System (RCS) in conjunction with the Integrated Development Environment.

Ê
ÌTo select a revision control system
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Choose Set Source Controlğ from the File menu.

A number of options are presented:

Ê
ÌMKS RCS
¹ ìMKS RCSì Mortice Kern Systems Revision Control System

ÌMKS SI
¹ ìMKS SIì Mortice Kern Systems Source Integrity

ÌPVCS
¹ ìPVCSì Intersolv Project Version Control System

ÌOther
¹ ìgeneric RCSì Any other flavour of revision control system (default)

ÌNone
¹ No revision control system

ÌObject Cycle
¹ ìObject Cycleì Powersoft's Object Cycle

ÌPerforce
¹ ìPerforceì Perforce
Ë

Ë

ìcheckout sourceì ìcheckin sourceì When you select a source revision control system, the Checkout Source and Checkin Source items in the Sources menu are enabled.  The Checkout Source and Checkin Source items spawn batch/command files or dialogs corresponding to your selection of revision control system.  The batch/command files are located in the \WATCOM\BINW and \WATCOM\BINP directories.  These files can be modified to suit your environment.

Ê
ÌMKS RCS
¹

Ê
ÌMKS_CO
¹ for checking out/locking files with MKS RCS

ÌMKS_CI
¹ for checking in/unlocking files with MKS RCS
Ë

ÌMKS SI
¹

Ê
ÌCheckout Dialog
¹ a check out dialog will appear on the screen when using MKS SI

ÌCheckin Dialog
¹ a check in dialog will appear on the screen when using MKS SI
Ë

ÌPVCS
¹

Ê
ÌPVCS_CO
¹ for checking out/locking files with Intersolv PVCS

ÌPVCS_CI
¹ for checking in/unlocking files with Intersolv PVCS
Ë

ÌOther
¹

Ê
ÌGEN_CO
¹ a file which you can edit to provide check out (and lock) functionality to any generic revision control system

ÌGEN_CI
¹ a file which you can edit to provide check in (and unlock) functionality to any generic revision control system
Ë

ÌNone
¹ When selected, Checkout Source and Checkin Source are disabled.
Ë

When one of the batch files is spawned, a number of arguments are passed to the batch file.

For checking out files, the following arguments are passed:

È
Ó "pause" or "nopause" to enable optional screen pause after completion of action

Ó full source file path specification

Ó full project file path specification (.wpj file)

Ó full target file path specification (.tgt file)
É

For checking in files, the following arguments are passed:

È
Ó full source file path specification

Ó full message file path specification (this file contains the textual message that you enter when checking in a file)

Ó full project file path specification (.wpj file)

Ó full target file path specification (.tgt file)
É
´
÷2÷Executing_Special_Commands÷Executing Special Commands÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îExecuting_Special_Commands

ìIntegrated Development Environment, before makeì ìbeforeì ìbefore makeì ìIntegrated Development Environment, after makeì ìafterì ìafter makeì Sometimes you may find it necessary to execute commands before and/or after performing a make.  The Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment provides Before and After commands in the File and Targets menus.  These commands enable you to perform actions immediately before or after making either the selected target or all targets for the current project.

Ê
ÌTo set Before or After directives:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Click once on the targetğ for which you want to set a before or after directive.

The title bar of the selected target is highlighted.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Choose Target Optionsğ from the Targets menu.

A submenu appears from the Target Options menu.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose Execute Before or Execute Afterğ from the Target Options submenu.

A Target Before or Target After dialog appears on your screen.

Ê
ÌNote:
¹ If you want to set before or after directives to be executed when you make all targets, select the before and after commands from the File menu.
Ë

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Enter the commandğ you want to execute before or after the selected target is made.

courier12°c°ide29.bmp°helv10
ïibïFigure 29.ğïibïğïbï Enter execute commands in the Target Before or Target After dialogs.ğ

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Click on OKğ to continue.

The IDE stores the information you entered and closes the dialog.  When you next perform a make on the selected target, the IDE will execute the before or after directive you entered.
Ë
´
÷2÷Adding_Resources_to_a_Target÷Adding Resources to a Target÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îAdding_Resources_to_a_Target

ìIntegrated Development Environment, adding resourcesì ìresources, addingì You may want to include resources in your target executable.  There are two ways of doing this in the IDE:  indirectly and directly.  The act of adding resources to a target can only be done for targets that support resources, such as Windows or OS/2 PM.

Ê
ÌTo add a resource to a target using the indirect method:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Create a new source fileğ with the extension ".rc".

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Click once on the new source fileğ to select it.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose Edit Textğ from the Sources menu to invoke the Open Watcom Editor.

The editor opens on your screen with an empty window.  You can now add the resource file text.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Enter the resource statementsğ in the editor.

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Save your work and close the editorğ when you are finished.

You return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now compile the new source file.

Ì(6)
¹ïbï Click once on the new source fileğ to select it.

The selected source file is highlighted in the target window.

Ì(7)
¹ïbï Choose Makeğ from the Sources menu to compile the new source file.

The IDE compiles the selected source file.  To do this, the IDE invokes the Resource Compiler which creates a ".res" file.  The ".res" file will be automatically included in the target when it is linked.
Ë

When you create the resource file directly, the source file contains the same information but it is stored in a different format.

Ê
ÌTo add a resource to a target using the direct method:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Create a new source fileğ with the extension ".res".

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Click once on the new source fileğ to select it.

Ì(3)
¹ïbï Choose Edit Resourcesğ from the Sources menu to invoke the Open Watcom Resource Editor.

The editor opens on your screen with an empty window.  You can now create the resources.

Ì(4)
¹ïbï Create the resources.ğ

Ì(5)
¹ïbï Save your work and close the editorğ when you are finished.

You return to the Open Watcom IDE Project window.  You can now compile the new source file.

Ì(6)
¹ïbï Click once on the new source fileğ to select it.

The selected source file is highlighted in the target window.

Ì(7)
¹ïbï Choose Makeğ from the Sources menu to compile the new source file.

The IDE compiles the selected source file.  The IDE does not need to invoke the Resource Compiler to create a ".res" file because you created the ".res" file directly using the Resource Editor.  The ".res" file will be automatically included in the target when it is linked.
Ë
´
÷2÷Including_Libraries_in_a_Target÷Including Libraries in a Target÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îIncluding_Libraries_in_a_Target

ìIntegrated Development Environment, adding librariesì ìlibraries, addingì You use the same procedure to include static and DLL libraries in your target as you use to add any source file to a target.  The IDE includes the libraries when the target is linked.

Ê
ÌTo create static and DLL libraries:
¹ 

Ì(1)
¹ïbï Create a new targetğ entering the library name in the Target Name field and choosing the appropriate image type.

Clicking on OK on the New Target dialog closes the dialog and creates a new target with the specified name in the title bar.

Ì(2)
¹ïbï Add the desired source files,ğ new or existing, to the library you have created.

Ê
ÌNote:
¹ If you want one of your other targets to use that library, you must enter the library as a source file for that target.  When you enter the library as a source file you must give it the extension ".lib", regardless of whether the library is a static or DLL library.  For example, if your library is entitled Target A and you want your existing Target B to use that library, add Target A as a source file on Target B with the extension ".lib".
Ë

Ë
´
