÷2÷Invoking_Other_Applications_from_the_IDE÷Invoking Other Applications from the IDE÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îInvoking_Other_Applications_from_the_IDE

ìIntegrated Development Environment, invoking toolsì ìinvoking toolsì When using the Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment, there are several other applications you may want to use.  To save you the frustration of having to constantly exit the IDE and start up a new application, we have made it possible to invoke other applications from within the Integrated Development Environment.  Following are the applications you can invoke from the IDE:

Òc
Ó Browser

Ó Open Watcom Debugger

Ó Open Watcom Execution Sampler

Ó Open Watcom Execution Profiler

Ó Open Watcom Disassembler

Ó Open Watcom Editor (text editor)

Ó Open Watcom Resource Editor

Ó Open Watcom Zoom

Ó Open Watcom Heap Walker

Ó Spy

Ó DDE Spy
Ô

You invoke these applications by making a selection from the toolbar or various menus in the IDE.  The Actions menu contains some of the applications.  You can find others in the Targets and Sources menus.  The menu options that appear vary, depending on the contents of the current project.  The applications are available in the menu items only when appropriate.  For example, if the currently selected target has no source files, no applications appear in the Sources menu.

ïibïHint:ğ  ìmouse, right buttonì ìright buttonì An alternate way to view the contents of the Targets and Sources menu is to click the right mouse button while your cursor is positioned in the project window.  If your cursor is positioned over a target, the Targets menu appears.  If your cursor is positioned over a source file, the Sources menu appears.

Refer to the appropriate sections in this manual for detailed information on using the various applications.
´
÷2÷Invoking_DOS_Applications_from_the_IDE_under_OSD2÷Invoking DOS Applications from the IDE under OS/2÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îInvoking_DOS_Applications_from_the_IDE_under_OSD2

ìDOS settingsì ìWIN-OS/2 settingsì There are occasions where you may wish to change the default DOS or WIN-OS/2 settings when running a DOS/Windows application from the IDE.  The mechanism which we provide is through the use of a specialïbï IDE_DOS_SETTINGSğ ìenvironment variables, IDE_DOS_SETTINGSì ìIDE_DOS_SETTINGS environment variableì environment variable.  For example, to set the DPMI memory limit to 16 and to enable DOS BREAK, you can set the environment variable as follows:

ïbïExample:ğ

courier12     set ide_dos_settings=dpmi_memory_limit#16;dos_break#1helv10

Due to the command processor limitations, the "#" character is used in place of the "=".  The above command is equivalent to configuring a DOS session with DOS settings as follows:

courier12      helv10
courier12     Variable            Valuehelv10
courier12     DPMI_MEMORY_LIMIT   16helv10
courier12     DOS_BREAK           ONhelv10
´
÷2÷Using_the_Information_of_the_IDE_from_outside÷Using the Information of the IDE from outside÷Open Watcom Integrated Development Environment
îUsing_the_Information_of_the_IDE_from_outside

ìIDE, makeì ìIDE2MAKE, Utilityì The batch utility IDE2MAKE generates .mk and .mk1 makefiles for use with Open Watcom Make from the IDE .wpj and .tgt files.  See IDE2MAKE in Open Watcom Tools User's Guide.

